@ source: arxiv 2607.02770

--- note
## Overall
Gemma 4 "follows a similar pre-training as Gemma 3." The report is deliberately high-level about data —
it does **not** give exact dataset sizes or token counts, so we report only what it states and invent
no numbers.

--- note
## Training data
The pre-training dataset is described as "a large-scale, diverse collection of data from a wide range of
domains and modalities, including **web documents, code, images, and audio** (audio for **E2B, E4B and
12B**), with a **cutoff date of January 2025**." That is the full description the report gives — no
mixture weights, no volumes.

--- note
## Tokenizer
Gemma 4 uses **the same tokenizer as Gemini** (Gemini Team, 2025): a **SentencePiece** tokenizer
(Kudo & Richardson, 2018) with **split digits**, **preserved whitespace**, and **byte-level
encodings**. The **vocabulary has 262k entries**. (Split digits help arithmetic; byte-level guarantees
no out-of-vocabulary text; preserved whitespace matters for code.)

--- note
## Filtering
Data is filtered to:

- **decontaminate benchmarks** (remove test-set leakage),
- reduce the risk of **unwanted or unsafe utterances**, and
- reduce the risk of **recitation** (verbatim memorised regurgitation).

Safety-oriented data pre-processing (e.g. removing certain personal information) is covered in g413.

